Hello dear all, I am estimating densities using Rdist, I have a very simple question. Section 5.3 of Buckland et al. 2015 states that a solution for a small sample size per stratum is to allow the scale parameter of the model to differ among strata. I am not sure how to do this. I would like to know if calling the ds function in the following way is enough or if something else is missing to change the scale parameter.

hr08_770<-ds (oct08, truncation = 770, key="hr", adjustment = NULL, formula = ~size + strata ,convert.units = 0.001 )

Where strata are the different stratum of my survey.
Kind regards
Antonela

From uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com  Mon Nov 25 14:35:55 2019
From: uf2122b34f1aa6e95 at groups.google.com (Eric Rexstad)
Date: Mon, 25 Nov 2019 14:35:55 +0000
Subject: Re: [distance-sampling] small sample size per stratum code
Message-ID: <x6GiUPIaLa4.dkeG6JeQBwAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <x6GiUPIaLa4.CLZwCiV8AQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
References: <x6GiUPIaLa4.CLZwCiV8AQAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/x6GiUPIaLa4/m/dkeG6JeQBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes Antonela

You will fit a single detection function with both group size and stratum as covariates with the code you have provided.  This is assuming you have a field in your data called "strata" and it represents the labels of your strata.  More likely, you have used "Region.Label" as the field naming your strata; if this is the case you would replace "strata" with "as.factor(Region.Label)" in your formula.

Be aware this model might create identifiability problems depending whether you are treating group size as a continuous or factor covariate.  Factor covariate models are more complex to fit, particularly when there are multiple covariates each with multiple levels.

