Eric,

Thanks for inspecting this result and offering these tips! I did not notice any convergence problems, however I was relying on ddf() to report a message or raise an exception if there was a problem. I had not thought to explicitly verify that there was not a convergence failure when the function succeeded and reported no messages. Should I be doing that?

However, I did not notice the other SEs after fixating on the huge one for Beaufort6. That does raise the question for me of whether the high SE for p and N can be attributed solely to the Beaufort6 problem, as I assumed, or somewhat to those other levels. If I have a chance to explore that in the future with this example, I will.

Back when I did it, in 2018, I did notice the problem of the overlapping fits of Beauforts 2-6, even though I did not notice the SEs you pointed out. At the time, I decided to bin them into categories 0-1, 2-3, and 4-6, based on having so few observations for 0 and being skeptical that it was so dramatically better than 1, and being able to discern some difference between medium and high states. The resulting detection function (below) did not exhibit nearly as high SEs. I am curious what you think about this one.

Thanks,

Jason

